================================================================================
TEST T35370 -- IPv4_DHCPClient - DHCP Extended ID
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 16:40-16:56 NZDT
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWPTCM-T35370. No steps exist for this case in the test-case database, and no
          functional spec exists for "Extended ID". Graded against the X960 platform support
          TPS row 30 (group "DHCP Client", Extended ID, priority 1, Yes), the X530 Software
          TPS grouping that places Extended ID beside Host Name as a client IDENTITY option,
          and the DUT's own CLI surface enumerated by read-only help probe before the test was
          designed (STEP 0). 6 steps as executed.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24 (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config, captured 16:55 at the group setup state
          `interface vlan48 / ip address dhcp`, after the legs; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- the DHCP client's whole extended-identity surface on this build works as
configured and is observable on the wire, each option absent in the baseline and each removed
cleanly by its no-form.
      option 61  client-id ............... PASS  (appears with RFC 2132 type byte 0x01 + the
                                                  interface MAC, length 7; absent in baseline)
      option 12  hostname ................ PASS  (carries exactly the configured string
                                                  "CKDHC1"; option 61 gone in the same frame)
      option 124 vendor-identifying-class  PASS  (enterprise number 207 = Allied Telesis)
      option 125 requested ............... PASS  (parameter request list gains exactly 125)
      removal of all four ................ PASS  (wire returns to the baseline row, and the
                                                  interface stanza to `ip address dhcp`)
CAVEAT:  All SVIs on this platform share one MAC (e01a.ead4.7043, proven in STEP 1), and
         `ip address dhcp client-id <IFNAME>` sends the MAC of the named interface. The CONTENT
         of option 61 therefore cannot distinguish one SVI from another on this DUT: `client-id
         vlan10` and `client-id vlan48` give byte-identical option 61. The discriminator used
         throughout is PRESENCE, TYPE BYTE and LENGTH of the option against its absence in the
         STEP 0 baseline, not a content comparison. That is a property of the platform's MAC
         allocation, not a fault in the command.
CAVEAT:  No functional spec for "Extended ID" could be found, so the case was graded against
         the whole identity surface the build offers rather than against a particular named
         option. A TPS "Yes" whose command was absent would have been a finding; none was.

