STEP 0 -- baseline (feature OFF)                                          done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The identity surface this build offers, enumerated read-only before anything was configured
  (tools/qmark.py types `PREFIX ?` with NO carriage return and clears the line with Ctrl-U;
  its self-test confirmed first that nothing gets executed):

    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp ?
      client-id  Specify the client identifier
      hostname   Specify the hostname
      <cr>
    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp client-id ?
      IFNAME  Interface name
    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp hostname ?
      WORD  Hostname for this DHCP client
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client ?
      default-route             Default route
      request                   Request an option
      vendor-identifying-class  Vendor-identifying-class
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client request ?
      vendor-identifying-specific  Vendor-identifying-specific
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class ?
      <cr>
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client default-route ?
      distance  Set the administrative distance
                                             <-- exactly four identity items: option 61,
                                                 option 12, option 124, and the request for
                                                 option 125. `default-route distance` is
                                                 routing, not identity, and is out of scope.

  The baseline leg, with plain `ip address dhcp` and nothing else configured.
  COUNTED: 7 DHCP frames on eth4; server decoded DISCOVER + REQUEST.

    DISCOVER chaddr=e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 ip_src=0.0.0.0
        opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43]
    REQUEST  chaddr=e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
        opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43]
        opt requested_addr = 192.168.48.100
        opt server_id      = 192.168.48.2
    leased: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 192.168.48.100
                                             <-- no option 61, no option 12, no option 124,
                                                 and no 125 in the request list. The client
                                                 leases successfully without any of them.

  This is the control every later step is measured against. Incidental, noted and not graded:
  the AW+ client's parameter request list is [1, 3, 15, 6, 43] -- subnet mask, router, domain
  name, DNS server and vendor-specific. The wiki DHCP page documents only 1/3/6/15, so
  option 43 is additional to that page's description.

STEP 1 -- option 61: "ip address dhcp client-id <IFNAME>"                 PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: configure the client identifier on vlan48, re-trigger the client, and read option 61
  out of the DISCOVER and REQUEST against its absence in STEP 0. Run twice, naming two
  different interfaces, to test whether the argument is observable on the wire.

  The command is REFUSED while a client is already running on the interface:

awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp client-id vlan10
  % DHCP client is already running on this interface.
                                             <-- a GENUINE rejection, not an advisory: cfg.py
                                                 exits 2 AND the configuration did not change

  The working sequence is to remove the client first and re-apply with the identifier:

awplus(config-if)# no ip address dhcp
awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp client-id vlan10
                                             <-- cfg.py exit 0, no "% " lines

  Verified configured before anything was measured, not assumed:

awplus# show running-config interface vlan48
  !
  interface vlan48
   ip address dhcp client-id vlan10
  !

  (Had the refused attempt been measured as it stood, the capture would have shown the DEFAULT
  client and read as "client-id has no effect" -- a false defect against TPS row 30.)

  Measured, leg `clientid` with `client-id vlan10`:

      DISCOVER opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043    (type 0x01 = Ethernet, then the 6-byte MAC)
      REQUEST  opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043

  Measured, leg `clientid48` with `client-id vlan48`:

      DISCOVER opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043    -- byte-identical

  The content discriminator is unavailable on this platform, and that is proven rather than
  asserted -- all SVIs share one MAC:

awplus# show interface vlan1 | include Hardware
  Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043
awplus# show interface vlan10 | include Hardware
  Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043
awplus# show interface vlan48 | include Hardware
  Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043

  => STEP 1 PASS: against a baseline in which option 61 is ABSENT, the option appears and is
     well-formed -- RFC 2132 hardware-type byte 0x01 followed by the 6-byte interface MAC,
     length 7. The two interfaces give identical content because every SVI on this platform
     carries the same MAC, so the <IFNAME> argument selects an interface whose address is
     indistinguishable from the others'. Presence, type and length are the discriminator, and
     all three are correct.

STEP 2 -- option 12: "ip address dhcp hostname <WORD>"                    PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config-if)# no ip address dhcp
awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp hostname CKDHC1
                                             <-- cfg.py exit 0

  Measured, leg `hostname`:

      DISCOVER opt hostname = 434b44484331 = "CKDHC1"
      and option 61 is ABSENT again (the command no longer carries client-id)

  => STEP 2 PASS: option 12 carries exactly the configured string, and the pair cuts both ways
     in one frame -- option 12 appears where it was absent in STEP 0, and option 61 disappears
     where STEP 1 had put it. That is the configuration driving the wire, not a coincidence.

STEP 3 -- option 124: "ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class"           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class
                                             <-- cfg.py exit 0

  Measured, leg `vic`:

      DISCOVER opt vendor_class = 000000cf 01 01
               (= DHCP option 124, Vendor-Identifying Vendor Class)

  => STEP 3 PASS: enterprise number 0x000000cf = 207, the IANA Private Enterprise Number for
     Allied Telesis, followed by a 1-byte data length and 1 byte of class data. Absent in the
     STEP 0 baseline, present here -- a clean matched pair with the correct enterprise number.

STEP 4 -- option 125: "ip dhcp-client request vendor-identifying-specific" PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client request vendor-identifying-specific
                                             <-- cfg.py exit 0

  Measured, leg `vis`:

      DISCOVER opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43, 125]
               opt vendor_class   = 000000cf0101   (still present from STEP 3)

  => STEP 4 PASS: the parameter request list gained exactly 125 (Vendor-Identifying
     Vendor-Specific Information) and nothing else, against the baseline's [1, 3, 15, 6, 43].
     Option 124 from STEP 3 is still carried, so the two commands are independent.

STEP 5 -- removal: every identity option returns to the baseline          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config-if)# no ip dhcp-client request vendor-identifying-specific
awplus(config-if)# no ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class
awplus(config-if)# no ip address dhcp
awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp
                                             <-- cfg.py exit 0, no "% " lines

  Measured, leg `removal`:

      DISCOVER opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43]     <- 125 gone
               opt requested_addr = 192.168.48.100
               (no client_id, no hostname, no vendor_class)

awplus# show running-config interface vlan48
  interface vlan48
   ip address dhcp

  => STEP 5 PASS: all four options are gone from the wire and the interface stanza is back to
     the group setup state. Removal is proven in frames first and in the running-config second,
     so the no-forms take effect on the protocol and not merely in the configuration text.

